      <h1 id="external-secrets-monitoring" class="group scroll-mt-20">Monitoring the External Secrets Operator for Red Hat OpenShift<a href="#external-secrets-monitoring" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h1>
      <p class="_abstract">By default, the External Secrets Operator for Red Hat OpenShift exposes metrics for the Operator and the operands. You can configure OpenShift Monitoring to collect these metrics by using the Prometheus Operator format.</p>
      <h2 id="external-secrets-enable-user-workload-monitor_external-secrets-monitoring" class="group scroll-mt-20">Enabling user workload monitoring<a href="#external-secrets-enable-user-workload-monitor_external-secrets-monitoring" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">By default, the OpenShift Container Platform monitoring stack does not scrape metrics from user-installed applications like the External Secrets Operator. Enabling user workload monitoring is necessary to collect critical operational data, such as synchronization status, API error rates, and controller performance. This helps you to configure custom alerts for secret sync failures and create dashboards to monitor the overall health of your secret management system. You can enable monitoring for user-defined projects by configuring user workload monitoring in the cluster. For more information, see "Setting up metrics collection for user-defined projects".</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul><li>You have access to the cluster as a user with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li></ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create the <code>cluster-monitoring-config.yaml</code> YAML file:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nkind: ConfigMap\nmetadata:\n  name: cluster-monitoring-config\n  namespace: openshift-monitoring\ndata:\n  config.yaml: |\n    enableUserWorkload: true"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Apply the <code>ConfigMap</code> by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f cluster-monitoring-config.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>
      Verify that the monitoring components for user workloads are running in the <code>openshift-user-workload-monitoring</code> namespace by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc -n openshift-user-workload-monitoring get pod"} lang="terminal" />
      <CodeBlock code={"NAME                                   READY   STATUS    RESTARTS   AGE\nprometheus-operator-5f79cff9c9-67pjb   2/2     Running   0          25h\nprometheus-user-workload-0             6/6     Running   0          25h\nthanos-ruler-user-workload-0           4/4     Running   0          25h"} lang="terminal" title="Example output" />
      <p>The status of the pods such as <code>prometheus-operator</code>, <code>prometheus-user-workload</code>, and <code>thanos-ruler-user-workload</code> must be <code>Running</code>.</p>
      </li>
      </ul>
      <section class="related-resources _additional-resources">
      <p class="related-resources-title">Additional resources</p>
      <ul><li><a href="https://docs.redhat.com/en/documentation/monitoring_stack_for_red_hat_openshift/latest/html/configuring_user_workload_monitoring/configuring-metrics-uwm#setting-up-metrics-collection-for-user-defined-projects_configuring-metrics-uwm">Setting up metrics collection for user-defined projects</a></li></ul>
      </section>
      <h2 id="external-secrets-enable-operator-metrics_external-secrets-monitoring" class="group scroll-mt-20">Configuring metrics collection for External Secrets Operator for Red Hat OpenShift by using a ServiceMonitor<a href="#external-secrets-enable-operator-metrics_external-secrets-monitoring" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">The External Secrets Operator for Red Hat OpenShift exposes metrics by default on port <code>8443</code> at the <code>/metrics</code> service endpoint. You can configure metrics collection for the Operator by creating a <code>ServiceMonitor</code> custom resource (CR) that enables the Prometheus Operator to collect custom metrics. For more information, see "Configuring user workload monitoring".</p>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul>
      <li>You have access to the cluster as a user with the <code>cluster-admin</code> role.</li>
      <li>You have installed the External Secrets Operator for Red Hat OpenShift.</li>
      <li>You have enabled the user workload monitoring.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Configure the Operator to use <code>HTTP</code> for the metrics server. <code>HTTPS</code> is enabled by default.
      <ol>
      <li>
      Update the subscription object for External Secrets Operator for Red Hat OpenShift to configure the <code>HTTP</code> protocol by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc -n external-secrets-operator patch subscription openshift-external-secrets-operator --type='merge' -p '{\"spec\":{\"config\":{\"env\":[{\"name\":\"METRICS_BIND_ADDRESS\",\"value\":\":8080\"}, {\"name\": \"METRICS_SECURE\", \"value\": \"false\"}]}}}'"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      To verify that the External Secrets Operator pod is redeployed and that the configured values for <code>METRICS_BIND_ADDRESS</code> and <code>METRICS_SECURE</code> are updated, run the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc set env --list deployment/external-secrets-operator-controller-manager -n external-secrets-operator | grep -e METRICS_BIND_ADDRESS -e METRICS_SECURE -e container"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>The following example shows that the <code>METRICS_BIND_ADDRESS</code> and <code>METRICS_SECURE</code> have been updated:</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"# deployments/external-secrets-operator-controller-manager, container manager\nMETRICS_BIND_ADDRESS=:8080\nMETRICS_SECURE=false"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>Secret</code> resource with the <code>kubernetes.io/service-account.name</code> annotation to inject the token required for authenticating with the metrics server.
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create the <code>secret-external-secrets-operator.yaml</code> YAML file:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Secret\nmetadata:\n  labels:\n    app: external-secrets-operator\n  name: external-secrets-operator-metrics-auth\n  namespace: external-secrets-operator\n  annotations:\n    kubernetes.io/service-account.name: external-secrets-operator-controller-manager\ntype: kubernetes.io/service-account-token"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>Secret</code> resource by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f secret-external-secrets-operator.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>ClusterRoleBinding</code> resource required for granting permissions to access metrics:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create the <code>clusterrolebinding-external-secrets.yaml</code> YAML file:
      <p>The following example shows a <code>clusterrolebinding-external-secrets.yaml</code> file.</p>
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1\nkind: ClusterRoleBinding\nmetadata:\n  labels:\n    app: external-secrets-operator\n  name: external-secrets-allow-metrics-access\nroleRef:\n  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io\n  kind: ClusterRole\n  name: external-secrets-operator-metrics-reader\nsubjects:\n  - kind: ServiceAccount\n    name: external-secrets-operator-controller-manager\n    namespace: external-secrets-operator"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>ClusterRoldeBinding</code> custom resource by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f clusterrolebinding-external-secrets.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>ServiceMonitor</code> CR if using the default <code>HTTPS</code>:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create the <code>servicemonitor-external-secrets-operator-https.yaml</code> YAML file:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1\nkind: ServiceMonitor\nmetadata:\n  labels:\n    app: external-secrets-operator\n  name: external-secrets-operator-metrics-monitor\n  namespace: external-secrets-operator\nspec:\n  endpoints:\n    - authorization:\n        credentials:\n          name: external-secrets-operator-metrics-auth\n          key: token\n        type: Bearer\n      interval: 60s\n      path: /metrics\n      port: metrics-https\n      scheme: https\n      scrapeTimeout: 30s\n      tlsConfig:\n        ca:\n          configMap:\n            name: openshift-service-ca.crt\n            key: service-ca.crt\n        serverName: external-secrets-operator-controller-manager-metrics-service.external-secrets-operator.svc.cluster.local\n  namespaceSelector:\n    matchNames:\n      - external-secrets-operator\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: external-secrets-operator\n      svc: external-secrets-operator-controller-manager-metrics-service"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>ServiceMonitor</code> CR by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f servicemonitor-external-secrets-operator-https.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>ServiceMonitor</code> CR if configured to use <code>HTTP</code>:
      <ol>
      <li>
      Create the <code>servicemonitor-external-secrets-operator-http.yaml</code> YAML file:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: monitoring.coreos.com/v1\nkind: ServiceMonitor\nmetadata:\n  labels:\n    app: external-secrets-operator\n  name: external-secrets-operator-metrics-monitor\n  namespace: external-secrets-operator\nspec:\n  endpoints:\n    - authorization:\n        credentials:\n          name: external-secrets-operator-metrics-auth\n          key: token\n        type: Bearer\n      interval: 60s\n      path: /metrics\n      port: metrics-http\n      scheme: http\n      scrapeTimeout: 30s\n  namespaceSelector:\n    matchNames:\n      - external-secrets-operator\n  selector:\n    matchLabels:\n      app: external-secrets-operator\n      svc: external-secrets-operator-controller-manager-metrics-service"} lang="yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>ServiceMonitor</code> CR by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc apply -f servicemonitor-external-secrets-operator-http.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      <p>After the <code>ServiceMonitor</code> CR is created, the user workload Prometheus instance begins metrics collection from the Operator. The collected metrics are labeled with <code>job="external-secrets-operator-controller-manager-metrics-service"</code>.</p>
      </li>
      </ol>
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>In the OpenShift Container Platform web console, navigate to <strong>Observe</strong> → <strong>Targets</strong>.</li>
      <li>
      In the Label filter field, enter the following labels to filter the metrics targets for each operand:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ service=external-secrets-operator-controller-manager-metrics-service"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>Confirm that the <strong>Status</strong> column shows <code>Up</code> for the <code>external-secrets-operator</code>.</li>
